WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.840
Staples like wheat, maize and rice

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.743
feed the world, providing
nearly half the calories we eat.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.890
But in many regions these crops

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.203
and our access to them are under threat.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.510
There's definitely an
urgency to find alternatives

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.160
to the current food systems,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.220
especially in the current
context that we're in now

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.130
where you've had supply
chains disrupted by Covid,

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.080
where you have the Ukraine - Russia war

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.470
which has reduced the
amount of wheat available,

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.840
where you have climate change

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.023
making it more difficult
to access water sources.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.560
Current global conditions have created

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.073
the worst food security crisis
in West Africa for a decade.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.420
And nearly 40 million
people in the Sahel region,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.233
a semi-arid belt across North
Africa are now facing hunger.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.840
But fonio, a small grain
native to that region

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.790
has the potential to help.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.730
In many ways fonio is
the crop of the future

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.650
but it's unable to live to that potential

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.250
because of how much it's
been neglected in the past.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.640
It tastes like couscous, but
what it has over couscous

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.850
is that it's gluten free,
it's easy to digest.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.050
It's protein rich,
anti-glycemic, which means

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:32.066
that it helps fight diabetes
and it's also very filling.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.470
But most importantly,

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.623
fonio is drought resistant
and can grow in poor soil.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.580
It only grows in the Sahel region

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.413
and a belt of Savannah below it.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.400
It's cultivated mainly for
sustenance in lean times

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.583
by local communities
like the nomadic Fulani.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.870
Fulani is really one of the
predominant ethnic groups

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:55.773
in the Sahel.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:01.820
Chef Fatmata Binta is from
Sierra Leone and based in Ghana

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.740
and so she's of that ethnicity.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.740
She was a refugee and ate fonio
to survive with her family.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.310
I want to decolonize African cuisine

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.470
because the questions I was asking myself

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.750
when I was in culinary school was,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:22.053
why am I cooking the food
I didn't grow up eating?

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:23.970
It didn't make sense to me.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.270
Also why food should travel miles to come

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.763
to my kitchen before
I serve it to someone?

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.570
After going to culinary school in Kenya

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.730
and working at top hotels in Accra,

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.330
Chef Fatmata Binta toured the
world with a pop-up restaurant

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.880
that gave others a chance
to taste her culture

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.463
with a focus on fonio.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.940
It's an ancient grain it has been around

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.750
for a very long time, we
believe over 7,000 years.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.220
It's very easy to grow.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.740
So if you think about a sustainable grain,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.580
that's the first grain that
will come to mind for me.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.193
You don't need to plow the
land too much to grow it.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.823
And it's a tiny grain
like the size of sand.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.250
The size of the fonio grains is a problem.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.110
Being so small and wrapped in a husk

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.270
that has to be removed before it's eaten

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:21.811
makes it extremely hard
and tedious to process.

00:03:21.811 --> 00:03:25.020
And in most places the
processing hasn't changed

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:25.853
for millennia.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:34.083
It starts with separating
the grain from the stem.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:36.840
The women will stomp on it

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.700
while they're singing and dancing.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.400
That's their way of also, you know

00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:43.163
having that community sisterhood, bonding.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.310
They're trying to get
the sand out of the fonio

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.280
after stomping

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:53.071
and then they will pound
it, they'll toast it.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:57.333
It takes like a week or two to process.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.840
Technological innovation
is a must if fonio

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.770
is ever to be a serious contender
as a crop of the future.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:11.340
There are only 700,000 tons
of it produced annually,

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.403
compared to over 700
million tons of wheat.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:19.143
About 75% of all fonio comes from Guinea.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.800
At a point, the former
president of Guinea,

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.590
President Alpha Condé, who was
actually deposed in a coup,

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.530
became a big advocate for fonio.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.160
He used to travel to neighboring countries

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.400
on diplomatic missions
and carried a bag of fonio

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.370
to give to his counterparts
because he recognized

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:39.780
that there was an export market

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.870
and that he could earn foreign
currency by exporting fonio.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:45.437
And so there was a push
to mechanize fonio.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.937
About a dozen countries can grow fonio,

00:04:56.937 --> 00:05:00.330
but in Guinea they actually
have quite a few mills

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.630
which is not the case
for the other countries.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:12.330
Those machines are very
helpful cause it helps us

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.471
to increase our productivity.

00:05:15.471 --> 00:05:20.422
Before the machines for
the production of one ton

00:05:20.422 --> 00:05:22.746
we could take three weeks.

00:05:22.746 --> 00:05:24.750
Now that we have the machines

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.993
we can just make three days or four days.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.010
But even mills in Guinea's capital,

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.113
Conakry haven't mechanized
the entire process.

00:05:35.113 --> 00:05:38.924
And so de-husking often still
happens the traditional way.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:44.010
Fonio's kind of got a
reputation in western media

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:48.180
as this super food that
had this quinoa potential.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.253
But it hasn't really taken off.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.402
There is not enough fonio being grown

00:05:54.402 --> 00:05:56.790
at least at a commercial level,

00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:00.150
and there's not enough
processing facilities.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.202
Fonio is actually considered a neglected

00:06:03.202 --> 00:06:04.440
and underutilized crop species,

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.636
that's actually a scientific category

00:06:06.636 --> 00:06:09.030
which is also called an orphan crop

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:11.973
which means it's been an
under-researched crop.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.570
More research could help improve yields

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:18.839
and bring the production
process into the 21st century.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:23.600
But to gain more traction,
ambassadors like Chef Binta

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:27.570
are crucial to spreading the
word about fonio's potential.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:29.190
There's something so powerful about food

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:33.660
and I believe it can break down barriers.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:34.773
It's a bridge.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.940
Like I get to collaborate a
lot with Michelin star chefs.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.760
I will send them fonio
and then challenge them

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.500
to create something because I believe

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:46.110
that's how you spark conversation

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.667
and that's how you educate people

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:49.773
and and expose it to the world.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:57.544
Local exposure is also crucial.

00:06:57.544 --> 00:07:00.480
Even though fonio grows in northern Ghana,

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.940
in the region's biggest city there, Tamale

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:04.863
the grain is nowhere to be found.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.414
It's been so much a grain that's been kept

00:07:08.414 --> 00:07:11.820
for farmer households but
even though I describe it

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:16.560
as a farmer crop, it's actually
very expensive to buy fonio.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.930
It's about eight times
more expensive than rice

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.090
at the same quantity of rice.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.700
And that's why if you
wanna buy it as an outsider

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.050
it's actually very expensive.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:27.723
It's a ceremonial meal.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:34.410
The idea is that if there's
a commercialization of fonio

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:35.820
what would also happen is that

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.910
the cost of fonio would go down.

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:42.090
But there needs to be
a value chain for fonio

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:46.890
for there to be more
players, more manufacturers,

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:48.603
more people who grow it.

00:07:50.153 --> 00:07:53.520
If you don't change mindsets
quickly and bring investment

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:56.407
people are actually
going to forget the skill

00:07:56.407 --> 00:07:57.960
because it's only traditional.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.480
And they needs to be a point where people

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.770
both researchers, industry,
governments, decide

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:08.133
that they need to invest
in this as a real solution.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.460
Chef Binta is one of many who

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.730
are taking fonio seriously,
so seriously in fact,

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.580
she's willing to bet her
own livelihood on it.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.880
She's now moving to Tamale,
to work directly with farmers

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:26.548
and try to build the value
chain from the ground up.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.920
I'll spend days with the locals

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:34.290
the keepers of the authentic recipes.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:36.180
And these women take time to teach me.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:38.093
They are passing down knowledge,

00:08:38.093 --> 00:08:40.830
they are passing down
sustainable traditions

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:44.520
and it didn't feel right
to walk away, go back

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:49.140
to my kitchen and not recognize
what they are going through.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:50.760
So I wanted to collaborate with them

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.040
in a more meaningful way.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.260
So the main goal is to partner

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.768
with women who are growing fonio already

00:08:57.768 --> 00:08:59.310
in the north of Ghana.

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:03.540
And we'll have a center where
we'll process, we'll package

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.910
and then we'll distribute
locally and internationally.

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:07.860
And I believe we have to start locally

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:10.466
because we have to place
value first locally

00:09:10.466 --> 00:09:12.033
and then we can scale it.

